 \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"75ba09bf47485fbc24781e9cf7d35431c5be544bfe370e60789b36e335d46ede","event_uuid":"b80c3458-6047-4258-8b80-ac407a91b4a3","position":{"commit_sha":"6bc799d7d6d4500c41c85e3215c36964084e2214","position":"1788430818270226001-b80c3458-6047-4258-8b80-ac407a91b4a3"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"fab2473caeb97e4e8f634011fe985c8c7ab607f3ca26f9c3e731eddb947de19f","event_uuid":"893644c9-d75d-4dfe-a60b-dad01e5be441","position":{"commit_sha":"ff37645e42b0a9e9aaa6a07864655ddf7469bc8b","position":"1788432264423133001-893644c9-d75d-4dfe-a60b-dad01e5be441"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"3b54377377f47de9ea22c07ed8cc6a5270df2b692b9f116aebe5eb5edb86278a","event_uuid":"cae6717e-8341-4593-9dcb-85850f8166c2","position":{"commit_sha":"7035338f7476b8fac98be27da710d37388d39d1f","position":"1788433119962672523-cae6717e-8341-4593-9dcb-85850f8166c2"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Implemented canonical bridge diagnostics migration for the remaining reconcile-check observables. Added fsck compatibility buckets for orphaned bindings/Jira and unbound locals, surfaced persisted reconcile-check summaries through bridge_status (including relocated store coverage), and updated the MCP bridge-status output model/schema mirror for the new reconcile_diagnostics field. Validation: targeted fsck/status/schema/MCP suites passed; make lint and make typecheck passed; pytest -m 'not integration and not external' now fails only on the pre-existing baseline test_atomic_completion_close::test_relevant_remote_rejection_never_places_candidate_on_shared_head, which reproduces on clean origin/main.","timestamp":1788432264423133001}],"created_at":1788425626666764001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"cae6717e-8341-4593-9dcb-85850f8166c2","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"766a-47da-cdb8-4c64"}],"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`reconcile-check` still packages several distinct observables together: field-diff discrepancies, orphaned bindings, unbound counts, summary tallies, text formatting, exit behavior, and the persisted report. The legacy follow-on removals in `82bb` and `c195` both depend on first proving which of those observations belong to `bridge preview`, `bridge fsck`, or `bridge status`, whether any observable truly lacks a home among those three existing owners, and how the remaining compatibility consumer in `src/rebar/_bridge_runner.py` behaves while the legacy mode string still exists.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Add RED-first characterization coverage that captures the current `reconcile-check` contract for healthy, discrepant, orphaned, and unbound cases, including the fact that a successful run exits 0 regardless of findings and only unexpected failures exit 1.\n2. Build an observable-to-owner matrix that assigns each current `reconcile-check` observation to an existing canonical bridge owner:\n   - `bridge preview`: field-diff discrepancies and their preview-oriented formatting\n   - `bridge fsck`: orphaned bindings, orphaned Jira references, unbound local/Jira counts, and integrity-oriented formatting\n   - `bridge status`: summary tallies, operational witnesses, and the persisted-report replacement metadata\n   - exit behavior: no new aggregate exit is introduced; each canonical owner keeps its pre-existing native exit contract, and the migration documentation records that `reconcile-check` itself did not have a findings-driven exit signal to preserve\n3. Implement the bridge-side routing needed to expose those preserved observables through the canonical owners. Introduce a new bridge diagnostic subcommand only if the observable-to-owner matrix proves that a named current observable cannot be expressed through preview, fsck, or status; if that happens, record the forcing observable and rationale in the ticket comment and migration doc.\n4. Migrate persisted report construction away from `bridge_state/reconcile-check.json` by defaulting to status-owned metadata/result fields rather than a new standalone file. Only keep or dual-write an on-disk artifact if a named consumer such as `_bridge_runner` or a held-out test proves it still needs one during the compatibility window before `c195` contracts the surface.\n5. Preserve `_bridge_runner` compatibility in this ticket: its `MODE_COMMANDS['reconcile-check']` entry and captured stdout remain intact unless the characterization matrix proves a bridge-owned replacement can preserve the observed contract without widening scope. Any actual mode-string or wrapper removal stays deferred to `c195`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- direct `reconcile-check` report construction and output shaping in `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler`\n- canonical `bridge preview` / `bridge fsck` / `bridge status` CLI result contracts in `src/rebar/_cli`\n- `_bridge_runner` compatibility behavior while `reconcile-check` is still a runnable profile\n- bridge diagnostics surfaced through library/MCP shims only where needed to preserve the canonical bridge result contract before `c195` removes compatibility entrypoints\n- migration docs and characterization tests covering healthy, discrepant, orphaned, and unbound cases\n\n## Testing\n\nWrite RED-first characterization tests for the current `reconcile-check` report fields, formatting, persisted output, `_bridge_runner` wrapper behavior, and exit-0-on-findings contract. After the migration, prove that preview, fsck, and status expose the preserved diagnostics at their assigned owners; that any retired field/output is absent with explicit migration evidence; that `_bridge_runner` still preserves its observed compatibility behavior until `c195`; and that each owner retains its native exit contract without inventing a new aggregate reconcile-check exit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every current `reconcile-check` observable is assigned to `bridge preview`, `bridge fsck`, or `bridge status`; if any observable is retired instead, the migration doc and ticket comment name the retired observable, rationale, replacement owner or explicit absence, and the characterization assertion that proves the retirement.\n- [ ] `bridge preview` exposes field-diff discrepancies and preview-specific formatting under its existing result contract.\n- [ ] `bridge fsck` exposes orphaned-binding, orphaned-Jira, unbound-count, and integrity-formatting diagnostics under its existing result contract.\n- [ ] `bridge status` exposes summary tallies, operational witnesses, and the persisted-report replacement metadata under stable names; status-owned metadata is the default replacement unless a named consumer proves an on-disk artifact is still required during the compatibility window.\n- [ ] The migration preserves the source fact that `reconcile-check` had no findings-driven exit signal: preview/fsck/status keep their existing native exits, and the migration doc plus characterization coverage record that there is no new aggregate exit parity contract to preserve.\n- [ ] `_bridge_runner` continues to invoke the legacy `reconcile-check` profile and preserves its observed compatibility output unless a bridge-owned replacement is proven equivalent within this ticket's scope.\n- [ ] A new bridge diagnostic subcommand is introduced only if the observable-to-owner matrix proves a named current observable has no home in preview/fsck/status, and the ticket comment records that forcing case.\n- [ ] Characterization and canonical bridge tests cover healthy, discrepant, orphaned, and unbound cases, including the compatibility wrapper behavior.\n","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/last_pass.py","reason":"surface persisted reconcile-check diagnostics through bridge status under canonical bridge ownership"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine_support/bridge_fsck.py","reason":"map legacy reconcile-check orphan and unbound observables onto canonical bridge fsck diagnostics"},{"path":"src/rebar/_mcp_models.py","reason":"keep MCP bridge-status output models aligned with the public schema after adding reconcile diagnostics"},{"path":"src/rebar/types.py","reason":"declare the additive bridge_status reconcile_diagnostics facade field"},{"path":"src/rebar/schemas/bridge_fsck.schema.json","reason":"document the additive fsck compatibility buckets for legacy reconcile-check observables"},{"path":"src/rebar/schemas/bridge_status.schema.json","reason":"document the additive bridge-status reconcile_diagnostics summary field"},{"path":"tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_fsck_binding_drift.py","reason":"cover fsck compatibility buckets and human-readable reporting for migrated reconcile-check observables"},{"path":"tests/interfaces/facades/test_bridge_status_relocated_store.py","reason":"prove bridge_status reads persisted reconcile-check diagnostics from the configured store path"}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["parent","3b66-615c-fbfb-4b1f"],["relates_to","766a-47da-cdb8-4c64"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":"3b66-615c-fbfb-4b1f","parent_status_uuid":"5a932d79-951e-43a2-ae4f-c2f1bbc2e8f7","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":1,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"in_progress","tags":["janitor","reconciler","bridge","migration"],"ticket_id":"bec7-d2ff-79d0-40ff","ticket_type":"task","title":"Canonicalize reconcile-check diagnostics","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["8389419b-08a6-4b53-911d-54a3af542d5c","ce3a2d96-616f-4586-84b7-0b370738147d","50eadcfd-0744-4f83-9372-eb79b5a27e49","83acf60e-469e-4627-84a6-9446bcc33f2c","051bc2b2-f8aa-4a65-a473-b254ea625e50","bad67744-7bd6-4573-8087-45b963221905","5a932d79-951e-43a2-ae4f-c2f1bbc2e8f7","31f036ec-417e-42e9-bc54-793479ff6f4d","9910202c-04d0-4771-befd-38e8b5c634e1","b80c3458-6047-4258-8b80-ac407a91b4a3","893644c9-d75d-4dfe-a60b-dad01e5be441","cae6717e-8341-4593-9dcb-85850f8166c2"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1788437994108400948,"uuid":"00c44846-6ff1-4496-a8c1-ce83b7835d24"}